Jahshaka patch 0032 — GPU-driven compute dispatch (vkCmdDispatchIndirect), and
the compute-write -> indirect-read barrier the BarrierSolver cannot express.

WHY (Photon lane S0; SPECS/NANITE_SPEC.md §4.2, SPECS/research/
LUMEN_SUPPORTING_TECH_2026-09-13.md §6; ledger §190/§192). Every Lumen-shaped
stage narrows its work as it goes: screen traces that miss are compacted into a
shorter list, that list is re-traced, what misses again is compacted once more.
Epic: "it was essential that we utilize indirect dispatch where supported",
worth up to a 50% tracing speedup from the compaction it enables. Without it
every later pass must be dispatched at its WORST CASE, because the CPU cannot
know how many items survived. It is the single mandatory piece of supporting
technology for either Photon arm, and this pin has none of it.

WHAT THE PIN HAS. `VulkanRenderSystem::_dispatch` issues `vkCmdDispatch` with
CPU-side group counts and nothing else; `vkCmdDispatchIndirect` appears nowhere
in the tree. `HlmsComputeJob::setNumThreadGroupsBasedOn` looks like the feature
and is not: it derives the counts on the CPU from a bound texture's or UAV's
DIMENSIONS (OgreHlmsComputeJob.cpp:699-744), so it can never see a number a
shader computed.

WHAT THIS ADDS, in four small pieces:

 1. `HlmsComputeJob::setIndirectDispatchBuffer( BufferPacked *, offsetBytes,
    issueBarrier = true )`. While a buffer is set the job dispatches indirectly
    and its CPU-side group counts are ignored. The buffer must be GPU-resident
    and readable as an indirect argument — in practice a `UavBufferPacked`,
    because the producing shader has to bind it as an SSBO to write the counts.
    NOTE that Ogre's own `IndirectBufferPacked` is NOT usable here on Vulkan:
    `VulkanVaoManager` forces `mSupportsIndirectBuffers` to false
    (OgreVulkanVaoManager.cpp:183-184) and emulates indirect DRAW buffers in
    system memory, so such a buffer carries no BufferInterface at all. The
    ordinary VBO pools are already created with
    `VK_BUFFER_USAGE_INDIRECT_BUFFER_BIT` (same file, :1122-1127), so a UAV
    buffer needs no allocator change.

 2. `RenderSystem::_dispatchIndirect` + `supportsIndirectDispatch()`. The base
    implementation throws ERR_NOT_IMPLEMENTED and `supportsIndirectDispatch()`
    returns false, so D3D11, Metal, GL3Plus, GLES2 and NULL are untouched and
    compile unchanged; only Vulkan overrides both.

 3. `HlmsCompute::dispatch` routes to `_dispatchIndirect` when the job carries a
    buffer, and `HlmsCompute::compileShader` no longer requires a non-zero
    `num_thread_groups_*` for such a job. That requirement exists because Metal
    needs the counts on the C++ side; an indirectly dispatched job has none by
    definition, and forcing every caller to carry a meaningless dummy would be a
    trap. The threads-per-group half of the check is untouched.

 4. THE BARRIER, hand-rolled in `VulkanRenderSystem::_dispatchIndirect`.
    `executeResourceTransition`'s buffer branch only ever sets
    `VK_ACCESS_SHADER_READ/WRITE_BIT`, and `ogreToVkStageFlags` knows only the
    six shader stages (OgreVulkanRenderSystem.cpp, both in this file), so
    `VK_ACCESS_INDIRECT_COMMAND_READ_BIT` at `VK_PIPELINE_STAGE_DRAW_INDIRECT_BIT`
    is unreachable from the BarrierSolver — and `BarrierSolver::assumeTransition`
    exists only for textures, so it cannot even be told after the fact. The edge
    is therefore issued directly as a `VkBufferMemoryBarrier` over the twelve
    bytes actually read, and DELIBERATELY NOT registered with the solver: it is
    orthogonal to the state the solver tracks (the argument buffer's ordinary
    SSBO write/read transitions are still resolved the normal way by the
    caller), so the solver stays coherent by construction rather than by
    notification. Routing it through `executeResourceTransition` would also be
    wrong mechanically: that function begins with `endAllEncoders()`, which
    would close the compute encoder the dispatch is being recorded into.

    It covers BOTH producers — `SHADER_WRITE` at COMPUTE_SHADER and
    `TRANSFER_WRITE` at TRANSFER — because Ogre's copy encoder does not end with
    INDIRECT_COMMAND_READ in its destination mask either, so a CPU-seeded or
    freshly uploaded argument buffer is the same gap wearing a different hat.

MEASURED (suite `compute.indirect_dispatch`, RTX 4080 SUPER, Vulkan): job A
counts the non-zero entries of a 4096-long list and writes the group count; job B
is dispatched from it and stamps `gl_NumWorkGroups.x` into one slot per group. At
0, 7 and 4096 survivors the count written, the number of groups that ran and the
count the groups read all agree, and the output buffer is byte-identical to the
same job dispatched from a CPU-side count. With the barrier suppressed
(issueBarrier = false), Vulkan synchronization validation reports
SYNC-HAZARD-READ-AFTER-WRITE at `vkCmdDispatchIndirect` naming exactly "must
allow VK_ACCESS_2_INDIRECT_COMMAND_READ_BIT accesses at
VK_PIPELINE_STAGE_2_DRAW_INDIRECT_BIT"; with it, the layer is silent. The data
hazard itself did not reproduce on this driver in four runs — which is why the
barrier is unconditional rather than opt-in.

The two argument-buffer preconditions (4-byte alignment, 12 bytes in range) are
OGRE_EXCEPTs and not OGRE_ASSERT_LOWs on purpose: this engine ships
RelWithDebInfo, where OGRE_ASSERT_LOW compiles to `(void)0`, so the named failure
the API promises would never fire in any build anyone runs — the alternative is a
validation-layer message, or silence, from inside somebody's render loop.

ABI: `HlmsComputeJob` grows three members. This is a SOURCE patch: every tree
must re-run irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh.

